<Layout title="Azure Credential Forwarding" description="Use DefaultAzureCredential inside Docker containers during local development with automatic credential forwarding via mTLS.">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <!-- Breadcrumb -->
    <nav class="text-sm mb-8">
      <ol class="flex items-center gap-2 text-neutral-500">
        <li><a href="/azd-app/" class="hover:text-blue-500">Home</a></li>
        <li>/</li>
        <li><a href="/azd-app/reference/cli/" class="hover:text-blue-500">Reference</a></li>
        <li>/</li>
        <li class="text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">Credential Forwarding</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="mb-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Azure Credential Forwarding</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        Use <code class="px-2 py-1 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded">DefaultAzureCredential</code> inside Docker containers during local development. No configuration files, no service principals, no secrets.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The Problem -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Problem</h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 mb-4">
        If you've ever tried to use <code class="px-2 py-1 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded">DefaultAzureCredential</code> inside a Docker container during local development, you've hit this wall: <strong>it doesn't work</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 mb-4">
        The Azure Identity SDKs rely on host-level credentials — Azure CLI tokens, environment variables, managed identity endpoints — none of which exist inside a container. This is a <a href="https://github.com/Azure/azure-sdk/issues/19167" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">long-standing pain point</a> across every Azure SDK: .NET, Python, Java, Node.js, and Go.
      </p>
      <div class="p-6 bg-red-100 dark:bg-red-900/40 rounded-lg border border-red-300 dark:border-red-700 mb-4">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">What developers do instead (and why it's painful)</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Create service principals</strong> — Operational overhead, secrets to manage, and they expire</li>
          <li><strong>Use connection strings</strong> — Secrets in config files, hard to rotate, easy to leak</li>
          <li><strong>Mount ~/.azure into the container</strong> — Insecure, leaks entire Azure config</li>
          <li><strong>Skip Azure entirely in local dev</strong> — Dev/prod divergence, bugs caught late</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p class="text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        Credential forwarding solves this by securely proxying your host credentials into containers automatically.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Quick Start -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Quick Start</h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 mb-4">
        Add two lines to your <code class="px-2 py-1 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded">azure.yaml</code>:
      </p>
      <Code code={yamlQuickStart} lang="yaml" frame="code" />
      <p class="text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 mt-4 mb-4">
        Then run:
      </p>
      <Code code={runCommand} lang="bash" frame="terminal" />
      <div class="mt-6 p-6 bg-green-100 dark:bg-green-900/40 rounded-lg border border-green-300 dark:border-green-700">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">That's it.</h3>
        <p class="text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
          <code class="px-2 py-1 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded">DefaultAzureCredential</code> now works inside your container exactly as it does on your host machine. No service principals, no secrets, no environment variables to manage.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How It Works -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How It Works</h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 mb-4">
        Credential forwarding creates a secure bridge between the container and your host's Azure credentials using mutual TLS (mTLS).
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-6 mb-3 text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Architecture</h3>
      <Code code={architectureDiagram} lang="text" frame="code" />

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-8 mb-3 text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Step-by-Step Flow</h3>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="p-4 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-700">
          <div class="font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 mb-1">1. Generate ephemeral mTLS certificates</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
            When <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">azd app run</code> starts, it generates a fresh Certificate Authority (CA), server certificate, and client certificate using ECDSA P-256 keys. These certificates are valid for 24 hours and are never written to disk outside of the container volume mount.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-700">
          <div class="font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 mb-1">2. Start the mTLS auth server</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
            An HTTPS server starts on localhost that only accepts connections presenting a valid client certificate signed by the ephemeral CA. This server proxies token requests to the real <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">azd auth token</code> command on the host.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-700">
          <div class="font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 mb-1">3. Cross-compile the azd CLI shim</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
            A lightweight Go binary is compiled for Linux (the container's OS). This shim mimics the <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">azd auth token</code> CLI interface but forwards requests over mTLS to the host auth server instead of trying to authenticate directly.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-700">
          <div class="font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 mb-1">4. Configure the container</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
            For each service with <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">local.credentials: azd</code>, the container is started with: the shim binary volume-mounted at <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">/usr/local/bin/azd</code>, client certificates at <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">/run/secrets/azd-auth</code>, and environment variables (<code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">AZD_AUTH_*</code>) pointing the shim to the auth server.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-700">
          <div class="font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 mb-1">5. Token flow at runtime</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
            When your application code calls <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">DefaultAzureCredential</code>, the Azure SDK discovers the azd CLI on <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">PATH</code> and invokes <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">azd auth token</code> (the shim). The shim makes an mTLS request to the host auth server, which calls the real <code class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 dark:bg-neutral-700 rounded">azd auth token</code> and returns the Azure AD token to the container.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-8 mb-3 text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">The Shim in Detail</h3>
      <Code code={shimExample} lang="bash" frame="code" />
    </section>

